## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design latency-aware, privacy-preserving, multilingual systems that justify decisions rather than just detect. Emphasizes RAG, policy integration, and drift handling across many languages.

## Key Concepts
- Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) vs pure classifiers
- On-device first-pass with quantized transformers
- Privacy: differential privacy, encrypted policy KB, secure inference
- Multilingual handling and code-switching
- Drift detection, rollout strategies, rollback plans

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo architecture sketch
class Detector {
  constructor(){ /* load on-device model */ }
  async predict(msg){
    const lang = identifyLanguage(msg);
    const local = await runOnDevice(msg, lang);
    const refs = await fetchPolicyRefs(msg, lang); // retrieval
    const rationale = buildRAG(refs, local);
    const cloudOut = await cloudInfer(msg, rationale);
    return { label: cloudOut.label, rationale };
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you evaluate policy-ref quality and latency separately?
- How would you handle a new language with minimal data and ensure privacy material?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a practical, multilingual, on-device NLP component with concrete constraints and a simple evaluation path.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight NER: lexicon + rules, tiny ML fallback
- Multilingual tokenization: EN/ES/DE handling
- Taxonomy normalization and confidence scoring
- On-device privacy and rollout strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
function extractMentions(text, lexicon) {
  const mentions = [];
  for (const [word, label] of Object.entries(lexicon)) {
    if (text.includes(word)) mentions.push({ word, label });
  }
  return mentions;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle ambiguous mentions across languages?
- What metrics and thresholds would you use for the confidence score, and how would you validate them on-device?

## Why This Is Asked

Regulated, large-scale NLP systems require auditable provenance, per-tenant governance, and secure artifact management. This question probes how to architect for verifiable model provenance, drift detection, and safe rollouts across tenants and languages.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant enclaves and policy evaluation
- Cryptographic signing and KMS/HSM
- Immutable audit trail with Merkle proofs
- Data lineage and privacy controls
- Drift detection across tenants
- Safe rollout and rollback (canary/blue-green)
- Governance and auditability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: sign artifact with KMS and record hash in ledger
function signArtifact(artifact, kmsClient, ledger) {
  const hash = hashArtifact(artifact);
  const signature = kmsClient.sign(hash);
  ledger.append({ hash, signature, timestamp: Date.now() });
  return signature;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale provenance checks as tenants scale to thousands?
- How would you verify rollback integrity without downtime in a live channel?

## Why This Is Asked
Addresses real fintech needs: fraud detection in multilingual chats with real-time constraints, privacy considerations, and production-grade monitoring.

## Key Concepts
- Multilingual transformers (XLM-R, mBERT) vs lightweight baselines
- Per-language calibration of thresholds
- Privacy: Federated learning, differential privacy (DP-SGD), on-device inference
- Explainability: attention-based explanations, feature importances
- Drift and fairness monitoring in production

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo
const model = loadModel('xlmr-base');
function classify(text, lang){
  const tokens = tokenize(text, lang);
  return model.predict(tokens);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle code-switching and language detection in slow-resource environments?
- What metrics and dashboards would you use to monitor privacy guarantees and drift in production?
